serted, not demonstrated.","The plan's RED demonstration proves the regex REJECTS lookalikes, but rejecting lookalikes is a different claim from CodeQL recognizing the construct as a sanitizer that clears the alert."],"finding":"The plan asserts the lookahead-anchored regex clears CodeQL alert #88 (AC #9) but does not resolve whether CodeQL's py/incomplete-url-substring-sanitization dataflow model recognizes a regex .search() over the whole document as an adequate sanitizer — the cited prior art (revealable-ungracious-billygoat) cleared its alert with the DOCUMENTED parse-the-URL approach (urlsplit origin equality), which the plan deliberately declines to reuse.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f86fc1e734b30a7c5","impact":0.0,"is_primary":null,"location":"Approach section ('matching the strictness of the parsed-origin prior art without urlparse machinery') and Acceptance Criteria line 9 ('After the fix lands and CodeQL re-runs, alert #88 clears without dismissal')","norm_id":"n6e2924930588556e","priority":0.0,"reason":"low-validity","recalled":false,"scenarios":["The regex correctly rejects lookalikes but CodeQL still flags line 51 because its taint model does not model a regex match over free-form document text as sanitization, leaving AC #9 unmet after landing.","The author must then re-do the fix with the parse-based approach the prior art already proved, after a merge round-trip."],"severity":"none","suggested_fix":"Add a note that the regex-vs-parse choice was checked against how CodeQL scores the fix (e.g. confirm the anchored-regex form is a recognized barrier for this query, or fall back to the prior art's urlsplit-origin comparison — which is proven to reach state=fixed — if it is not).","tier":"LLM","validity":0.25,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"na","asserted_capability_confirmed":"na","cited_reference_accurate":"insufficient","claims_absence":"na","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"no","current_state_satisfie